#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133805 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133905 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Regression With Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134029 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6c1ae6c7-6771-5b39-80ac-beb1cde38bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07c1a1e5-eac4-585d-8460-669cc1e39819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134106 #edb/ref 1074 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72cc6e31-5631-5692-b09e-242241827364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134108 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1071 "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134111 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186134703 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134703 #edb/ref 1061 20307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ac1d0c2-c862-5d00-b1e2-ce846fd9a9e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134704 #edb/ref 1061 20308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "342fc855-0200-53f5-b531-d6f0f6dc12c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134705 #edb/ref 1061 20310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db5ddd01-2672-598e-9121-f4cf661cc836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134886 #edb/ref 1061 37273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af2c310b-8cc3-5365-a974-d47b29df8d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134888 #edb/ref 1061 37328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c7f5aff-10ef-54b2-b0e0-98663daec8b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135062 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135062 #edb/ref 1065 3652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135062 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "49c9c3ab-40d7-5e87-934c-1c3fcd16b330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135062 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135142 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value. The residuals for the data set we considered earlier are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/515aaaeef2f85bb131564b92bdaef9ef514d05c8028ae85382d231565d490665.png)\n\nIn the past, you may have seen that  calculating a regression line typically means finding a line where the sum of the squared residuals \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nUsing matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the condition that the sum of the squared residuals is minimized is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.$ This, in turn, is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.$ \n\nTherefore, the problem of minimizing the squared residuals is equivalent to solving the least squares problem for $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135142 #edb/ref 1065 6149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135142 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eeb5c2f5-02c9-5579-97b5-0393df974a32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135142 #edb/ref 1067 "Least-Squares vs. Minimizing the Sum of the Squared Residuals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269437 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-2+1.4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269437 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269437 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13253f23-aec5-55ad-b4c7-17df66b79ec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270160 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2-1.4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64399bb9-63ee-5478-9c09-041f12659f43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270211 #edb/ref 1080 "y=1.7-6x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270211 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6a2edf9f-25b1-5fd5-948c-237e6c5948da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270416 #edb/ref 1080 "y=1.4+2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270416 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84dd91b7-54c3-5219-9384-e547a61ebd85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270454 #edb/ref 1080 "y=6-1.7x" #edb