rend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186402736 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0007be5-4b39-5b13-9d0c-f56e380ce0f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402736 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402737 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ba3cb994-1bad-5429-8732-d77a5dada295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402738 #edb/ref 1080 "$$1\\,050$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402738 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402738 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e9ac6ff-6dc0-50d1-bcf0-e5924b445f0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402739 #edb/ref 1080 "$$950$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402739 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402739 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc84303a-bedd-5991-9237-200188eab34c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402740 #edb/ref 1080 "$$875$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402740 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402740 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc47e4af-c36a-5241-a8ef-baaa45ef2c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402741 #edb/ref 1080 "$$900$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402741 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402741 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402742 #edb/ref 1080 "$$850$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402742 #edb/ref 1082 #