beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186402749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186402747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402744 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dd4ac2e5-74a0-528f-8078-d6aa415b8f9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402745 #edb/ref 1080 "$$24\\,600$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402745 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402745 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ba73043-ba7f-5144-85cc-553d87d248c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186402746 #edb/ref 1080 "$$37